# sympsica ZT-5 goldens — distributed DPF key generation at 61 bits (W2.1 step 5)
# format: the same line-based "key value..." shape as the Phase-1 seed*.fixture
# files (see test/fixtures/README.md), read by test/utils/fixture_support.hpp.
#
# Unlike the Phase-1 fixtures these rows have NO Python golden source: there is
# no reference implementation of libOTe's interactive RegularDpf keyGen. They
# are RECORDED from the first green run of
#   integration.ZtGatePipeline.ZT5_DkgAt61Bits
# on the build variant named by `tag` below, which is what [POST-GATE] means in
# the task-5 brief. To regenerate, run that test: it prints every row it
# computes as `zt5 <seed> <r> <share_digest> <recon_digest>` on stdout, in
# order, whether or not the comparison passes.
#
# Columns of a `zt5` row:
#   seed          — the run's PRNG seed (party R uses block(seed,0), S block(seed,1))
#   r             — the accepted logical mask r = r_R XOR r_S, as an integer
#   share_digest  — FNV-1a over, for every one of the 4*2^16 leaves: party 0's
#                   raw F_p share, party 1's raw F_p share, and both tag bits.
#                   TRANSCRIPT-dependent: it pins the actual DPF key material,
#                   so it catches any drift in libOTe's keyGen, in the silent-OT
#                   pool feeding it, or in CoeffCtxFp61.
#   recon_digest  — FNV-1a over the RECONSTRUCTED value and tag of every leaf.
#                   Mathematically determined (1 at the active leaf, 0
#                   elsewhere), hence portable by construction.
#
# `tag` records the build variant the digests were recorded on, because
# CMakeLists.txt derives ENABLE_SSE from the host architecture and that selects
# which libOTe code paths compile. share_digest SHOULD still be portable — every
# step is exact integer/AES arithmetic seeded from fixed PRNG seeds, with no
# floating point and no thread-count dependence (silent OT runs single-threaded)
# — but that has only been VERIFIED on the tagged variant. The Linux/x86 leg is
# unverified here (it is Task 8 / the AWS leg's job); if share_digest diverges
# there while recon_digest still matches, the divergence is in libOTe's
# arch-specific code paths, not in this pipeline, and the fix is to record a
# second tagged set of rows rather than to relax the check.
format 1
tag arm64-nosse
domain 65536
num_points 4
zt5 0 15458130772355002 1781344865116801613 2094893528438900733
zt5 1 779871122590035184 3544529244379380399 5976150381805668861
zt5 2 1092673996342980394 14690265592744655441 14600403497099982797
zt5 3 446455522420466017 59468408583065159 2632387665357688797
zt5 4 1652564973126982044 14036287742333843065 16147988885925514429
zt5 5 371489256052942171 3304877837440511393 5032835049735108301
zt5 6 403468191995462136 3443786960840314487 16971011284877322029
zt5 7 1376233292255474441 17494842079376875645 1946069906623619453

# ============================================================================
# SECOND tagged row-set (Task 9, Linux/x86-64 leg closure via colima+QEMU).
# Recorded from integration.ZtGatePipeline.ZT5_DkgAt61Bits, run directly
# inside a linux/amd64 gcc:13 container (colima/QEMU x86_64 emulation on
# Apple Silicon), against sympsica_tests_integration built from commit
# 7862c8b0b7a4db7d298e08126ff5b1ba16318c99 (branch sdd/phase3) with
# SYMPSICA_SSE ON (default for x86-64, CMakeLists.txt's SYMPSICA_SSE option)
# -- i.e. build_tag() == "x86_64-sse". Captured by temporarily patching a
# SCRATCH COPY of this file's `tag` line to `x86_64-sse` (never the host
# tree) so the test's build-variant ASSERT would pass and let the rows
# print; the scratch tag edit was reverted afterward. See task-9-report.md
# for the full procedure.
#
# VERDICT: every (seed, r, share_digest, recon_digest) row below is
# BYTE-IDENTICAL to the arm64-nosse row for the same seed above -- only the
# `tag` differs. This confirms libOTe's RegularDpf keyGen, the silent-OT
# pool feeding it, and CoeffCtxFp61 all produce identical key material
# bit-for-bit whether SSE is off (arm64) or on (x86_64); the ENABLE_SSE-
# selected code paths inside libOTe/cryptoTools do not change this
# pipeline's output, only (presumably) its performance. Fixture::one()/
# ::all() (test/utils/fixture_support.hpp) are keyed by column name only,
# not by `tag`, so this block is not a live, test-selectable alternative --
# it is recorded here as pinned cross-architecture evidence, per the header
# note above ("record a second tagged set of rows"). The rows the running
# test actually checks against remain the arm64-nosse block above,
# unchanged, positionally first.
format 1
tag x86_64-sse
domain 65536
num_points 4
zt5 0 15458130772355002 1781344865116801613 2094893528438900733
zt5 1 779871122590035184 3544529244379380399 5976150381805668861
zt5 2 1092673996342980394 14690265592744655441 14600403497099982797
zt5 3 446455522420466017 59468408583065159 2632387665357688797
zt5 4 1652564973126982044 14036287742333843065 16147988885925514429
zt5 5 371489256052942171 3304877837440511393 5032835049735108301
zt5 6 403468191995462136 3443786960840314487 16971011284877322029
zt5 7 1376233292255474441 17494842079376875645 1946069906623619453
# ============================================================================
